Lab 17: Building Scalable Microservices with Camel
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Develop microservices using Apache Camel
Deploy and scale Camel-based microservices on Kubernetes/OpenShift
Monitor and optimize resource usage in containerized environments
Prerequisites
Basic knowledge of Java and Spring Boot
Docker installed (v20.10+)
Minikube or OpenShift CLI (oc) configured
Apache Camel 3.x dependencies
kubectl installed (for Kubernetes)
JDK 11+
Lab Setup
Install Required Tools

# Install Docker
sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install docker.io -y

# Install Minikube (for local Kubernetes)
curl -LO https://storage.googleapis.com/minikube/releases/latest/minikube-linux-amd64
sudo install minikube-linux-amd64 /usr/local/bin/minikube
minikube start --driver=docker

# Install kubectl
curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl"
sudo install -o root -g root -m 0755 kubectl /usr/local/bin/kubectl
Initialize a Camel Spring Boot Project
Use Spring Initializr or run:

curl https://start.spring.io/starter.zip -d dependencies=camel,web \
-d type=maven-project -d baseDir=camel-microservice -o camel-microservice.zip
unzip camel-microservice.zip && cd camel-microservice
Task 1: Develop Camel-Based Microservices
Subtask 1.1: Create a Simple Camel Route
Edit src/main/java/com/example/CamelRoute.java:

import org.apache.camel.builder.RouteBuilder;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class CamelRoute extends RouteBuilder {
    @Override
    public void configure() throws Exception {
        from("timer:foo?period=5000")
          .log("Microservice triggered at ${header.firedTime}");
    }
}
Expected Outcome:
The service logs a message every 5 seconds when started.

Subtask 1.2: Add REST Endpoint
Add Camel REST DSL to CamelRoute.java:

rest("/api")
  .get("/hello")
  .produces("text/plain")
  .route()
  .setBody(constant("Hello from Camel Microservice!"))
  .endRest();
Test the Endpoint:

./mvnw spring-boot:run
curl http://localhost:8080/api/hello
Expected Output: Hello from Camel Microservice!

Task 2: Scale Microservices with Kubernetes/OpenShift
Subtask 2.1: Containerize the Microservice
Create Dockerfile:

FROM eclipse-temurin:11-jre
COPY target/*.jar app.jar
ENTRYPOINT ["java","-jar","/app.jar"]
Build and push to Docker Hub:

./mvnw package
docker build -t yourusername/camel-microservice:1.0 .
docker push yourusername/camel-microservice:1.0
Subtask 2.2: Deploy to Kubernetes
Create deployment.yaml:

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: camel-microservice
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: camel-microservice
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: camel-microservice
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: camel
        image: yourusername/camel-microservice:1.0
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8080
Deploy and verify:

kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
kubectl get pods -w  # Watch scaling status
Task 3: Monitor and Optimize Resources
Subtask 3.1: Add Metrics with Prometheus
Add dependency to pom.xml:

<dependency>
  <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
  <artifactId>camel-micrometer</artifactId>
</dependency>
Configure metrics in application.properties:

management.endpoints.web.exposure.include=*
Subtask 3.2: Autoscaling
Create HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler):

kubectl autoscale deployment camel-microservice --cpu-percent=50 --min=2 --max=5
Generate load to test scaling:

kubectl run -i --tty load-generator --image=busybox -- sh
while true; do wget -q -O- http://camel-microservice:8080/api/hello; done
Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Built a Camel microservice with REST capabilities
Containerized and deployed it on Kubernetes with scaling
Implemented monitoring and autoscaling
Key Takeaways:

Camel integrates seamlessly with Spring Boot for microservices
Kubernetes provides declarative scaling for Camel routes
Resource monitoring is critical for production deployments
Troubleshooting Tips
If pods don't scale, check HPA events:
kubectl describe hpa camel-microservice
For Docker push errors, verify login:
docker login
